[h1]Shift Change[/h1]

[b]Requires the Odyssey expansion[/b] — the outfit stand this mod builds on is Odyssey content, so without it nothing happens at all. Harmony is also required.

Colonists change into the right clothes for the room they work in, and back out again afterwards.

[img]https://i.imgur.com/flyDb1M.gif[/img]

Put a vanilla outfit stand in a work room and put a work outfit on it. When a colonist takes on [b]automatic[/b] work of that room's kind — doctoring in the hospital, researching or synthesizing drugs in the lab, cooking in the kitchen — they change into the stand's outfit before starting, and change back when their work takes them elsewhere. Their own clothes wait in the stand and come back exactly as they were, force-worn markers included.

[h2]How to use it[/h2]

That is the whole setup. Two optional controls per stand:

[list]
[*][b]Shared (set owner)[/b] reserves a stand for one colonist. Left unassigned, a stand is shared by whoever is free to use it, like beds — so a kitchen needs one stand per cook working [i]at once[/i], not one per cook.
[*][b]The work-type switch[/b] — labelled with what the stand currently dresses for ("Shift stand: doctoring", "Not used for shift changes") — opens a checklist for rooms the game reads oddly, rooms that do two jobs, or a decorative stand you do not want used at all. The dialog also names what the game currently reads the room as, which explains the surprises: a crib in a hospital, for instance, makes the room a barracks as far as the game is concerned.
[/list]

[b]The stand's Allow removing items toggle holds itself off while the stand is in service.[/b] Shift changes never need it, and turning it on hands the stand's contents to every colonist's outfit optimizer — which may take the uniform and wear it as everyday clothes, and assigning an owner does not prevent that. A stand already switched on stays switchable, so there is always a way back. To let haulers empty a stand, set it to [b]Not used for shift changes[/b] first — the toggle unlocks with it.

A stand that is dressing anyone says so in its inspect pane: what work it dresses for, who owns or is currently wearing it, and whether it is empty.

[h2]The rules it follows[/h2]

Deliberately narrow, so it never fights you:

[list]
[*][b]Automatic work only.[/b] Ordering a doctor to tend someone right now never sends them across the base to change first, and a colonist already in uniform keeps it on for a direct order.
[*][b]Emergencies are never delayed[/b], and nothing happens while the map is under threat.
[*][b]Personal kit stays personal.[/b] Nobody takes clothes out of a stand somebody else is using.
[*]Passing through a room changes nothing — only doing the room's work does.
[*][b]A meal break gets them out of uniform first[/b], wherever the food is stored — unless it is already in their hands or their pack; that they just eat. Otherwise a cook carries a meal across the base in whites to reach a chair, which is the walk this mod exists to prevent.
[*]If a stand frees up while someone is already working in its room out of uniform, they step over and change — unless they are mid-treatment on a patient.
[*]A [b]Change back[/b] button appears on any colonist currently in a uniform, for when you want them out of it now.
[/list]

Rooms are read from their role, so hospitals, laboratories, kitchens, workshops and barns all work out of the box. [b]Set work types[/b] covers everything else.

One setting: [b]Unassigned stands are shared[/b], on by default. Turn it off and only stands with an explicit owner ever dress anyone.

[h2]What to put on the stand[/h2]

[b]Not required, and not a dependency[/b] — but worth knowing before you install. Vanilla has no scrubs, no lab coat and no chef's whites, so in a pure vanilla game there is very little to actually dress anyone [i]in[/i]. Vanilla Apparel Expanded adds exactly those, and they are what every screenshot here uses.

[url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=1814987817]Vanilla Apparel Expanded[/url] by Oskar Potocki, Trunken and Sarg.

Any apparel mod works. That one is simply the closest fit, and this mod is not affiliated with the Vanilla Expanded team in any way.

[h2]Mod compatibility[/h2]

[url=https://steamcommunity.com/workshop/filedetails/?id=3545172389]Outfit Stands Plus[/url] works alongside this mod, in either load order — tested with both. Its mechanized and mending stands are full shift stands (shift changes run at their boosted swap speeds, and the mending stand repairs a borrower's parked clothes while they work), and each stand shows exactly one Set owner control: this mod's while the stand is in service, theirs when the stand is set to Not used for shift changes. Its wardrobe features, its allow adding items toggle and its research are all untouched.

Two of its behaviours are worth knowing about, because they can look like faults here: it switches off its own allow adding items after a manual swap, which quietly stops haulers restocking that stand until it is switched back on; and its right-click Return to stand, used on a colonist who is mid-shift, moves clothes the shift system was tracking — nothing is lost, and the next change-back sorts it out.

Pick Up And Haul users may see a burst of red "could not reserve" messages when several freshly built stands are stocked at once. That is its batch hauling racing itself over the new containers — cosmetic, the items arrive, and it stops once the stands are full.

[h2]Save safety[/h2]

Add it to an existing save freely — your existing outfit stands gain the new controls on load, and nothing needs rebuilding. Removing it is also safe: stands revert to ordinary vanilla furniture, and a colonist who was mid-shift simply keeps the uniform ([b]Clear forced apparel[/b] on the Assign tab un-forces it) with their own clothes waiting in the stand.

[h2]Adds no art[/h2]

No clothing, no buildings, no textures. It adds behaviour to a building the game already draws, and its icons come from vanilla's own UI. The code and defs are written with AI assistance; there is no generated art anywhere, because there is no art at all.

[h2]It is tested[/h2]

Behaviour is covered by an automated suite that runs inside RimWorld itself. The cases that matter most are the ones that are hard to undo — a gravship flight, a colonist dying or being banished while wearing a stand's outfit — and they run before every release.

That does not make it bug-free. It means the specific ways this could lose your colonists' clothes fail loudly in testing instead of quietly in your save. The suite is in the repository, it runs with one command, and the notes say plainly what it does not cover.

[h2]Source[/h2]

MIT licensed, and the repository includes the technical design notes and the test suite, so none of it has to be taken on trust.

[url=https://github.com/beverage/shift-change]github.com/beverage/shift-change[/url]

[h2]Credit[/h2]

[url=https://github.com/aedbia/AutomaticSwapForStand]Automatic Swap Outfit[/url] by aedbia (MIT) covers adjacent ground — automatic swapping at an outfit stand, triggered by allowed-area boundaries. Shift Change is an independent implementation with a different trigger (the room and its work type) and per-stand ownership, but that mod is why several dead ends were cheap to avoid.

